WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.200
MySQL, in depth.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.823
It is everywhere in existing companies.

3
00:00:04.823 --> 00:00:08.933
Knowing where it truly differs from Postgres is a professional habit.

4
00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:15.500
The part of MySQL worth knowing deeply is InnoDB, its default storage engine.

5
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.533
It gives row-level locking and crash recovery.

6
00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:26.300
And MySQL has had a real JSON type since five point seven, so the claim that it cannot do documents is folklore.

7
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.033
Here is InnoDB's most distinctive trait.

8
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:33.633
A query first finds a value through a secondary index.

9
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:37.067
Each secondary index entry carries the primary key.

10
00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:42.967
And the table itself is physically stored in primary key order, so that key leads straight to the row.

11
00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:46.667
Now the differences you should verify yourself.

12
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:49.200
The upsert syntax is spelled differently.

13
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.718
Postgres has a full outer join.

14
00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:54.867
MySQL needs a union of left and right joins.

15
00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:57.973
Postgres can return changed rows inline.

16
00:00:57.973 --> 00:00:59.867
MySQL needs another select.

17
00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:04.133
Default isolation levels differ, and so do identifier quotes.

18
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:06.167
Run each one on both engines.

19
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:09.767
Here is the same upsert in both dialects.

20
00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:13.167
Postgres uses ON CONFLICT, naming the key column.

21
00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:16.633
MySQL uses ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE instead.

22
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:21.096
The difference that bites hardest is in migrations.

23
00:01:21.096 --> 00:01:26.533
MySQL auto-commits schema changes, so a failed migration can leave you half-migrated.

24
00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:29.166
Postgres rolls the whole thing back.

25
00:01:29.166 --> 00:01:31.433
On MySQL, plan for partial failure.

26
00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:34.033
Here is why it matters.

27
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:39.533
A client wants your ticket summarization model wired into their MySQL eight database.

28
00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:44.600
Your Postgres-flavored scripts fail on ON CONFLICT, and a rollback that never happened.

29
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.167
The engineer who knows the real differences ships that week.

30
00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:50.111
Let's recap.

31
00:01:50.111 --> 00:01:52.900
MySQL is everywhere, and you will inherit it.

32
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.733
InnoDB gives row locks and a clustered primary key.

33
00:01:57.033 --> 00:02:00.700
Know the real differences from Postgres, because you verified them.

34
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.085
And remember that MySQL auto-commits DDL.

35
00:02:04.085 --> 00:02:05.967
Next up: data warehouses.
